e pattern in the series. The series can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{3^n}{2 \\cdot 103^n}\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out the constant \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) from the series:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac{3}{103}\\right)^n\n\\]\n\nThis is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = \\frac{3}{103}\\) and the common ratio \\(r = \\frac{3}{103}\\). The sum of an infinite geometric series \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} ar^n\\) is given by \\(\\frac{a}{1-r}\\) for \\(|r| < 1\\). However, our series starts at \\(n=1\\), so we need to adjust the formula accordingly:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac{3}{103}\\right)^n = \\frac{\\frac{3}{103}}{1 - \\frac{3}{103}} = \\frac{\\frac{3}{103}}{\\frac{100}{103}} = \\frac{3}{100}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{100} = \\frac{3}{200}\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(\\frac{3}{200}\\) as a terminating decimal, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{200} = 0.015\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.015}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0.015}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:871"}
